  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I checked the dashboard and the rule by reading them back from Kibana.\n\n**1. Data view.** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) uses **`timestamp`** as its time field. Its main fields are:\n- **Flight:** `FlightNum`, `Carrier`, `Cancelled` (true/false), `FlightDelay` (true/false), `FlightDelayMin`, `FlightDelayType`, `FlightTimeMin`, `FlightTimeHour`, `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, `DistanceMiles`\n- **Origin:** `Origin`, `OriginAirportID`, `OriginCityName`, `OriginCountry`, `OriginRegion`, `OriginWeather`, `OriginLocation` (map point)\n- **Destination:** the same set with a `Dest` prefix, such as `DestCityName` and `DestLocation`\n- **Time:** `timestamp`, `dayOfWeek`, `hour_of_day`\n\n**2. Dashboard.** 'S4-ef4329b2 Flights' (id `2c3eb9a9-cf7a-4c7f-9449-b6d53beadc4c`) has exactly two panels, both on the flights data view:\n- a line chart of the number of flights over time;\n- a bar chart of the number of flights for each of the top 10 values of `Carrier`.\n\n**3. Alert rule.** 'S4-ef4329b2 Cancelled flights' (id `9506d3b6-d161-46fe-bcaf-77f14c195ec4`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_flights`. It runs every minute, counts flights where `Cancelled` is true over the last 5 minutes, and fires when the count is more than 10. It has no actions, so it won't send notifications when it fires. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to turn it on.\n\n**4. Edits and checks.** I renamed the carrier panel to 'Flights by carrier'. Reading the dashboard back shows both panels: 'Flights over time' and 'Flights by carrier'. Reading the rule back shows it is enabled, with a 1-minute schedule and a successful last run.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
